---
import "../styles/global.css";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import LinkButton from "../components/LinkButton.astro";
import { REPO, docLink } from "../data/site";

// The context figure, quoted with its source. It belongs here and not in
// the hero: it explains why the project exists, it isn't a claim about
// Orbit, and a statistic in a headline reads as a pitch.
const context = {
  headline: "Data centres used about 415 TWh in 2024, around 1.5% of global electricity.",
  growth: "Demand has grown about 12% a year since 2017.",
  source: "IEA Energy and AI",
  sourceHref: "https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai",
};

const beliefs = [
  {
    title: "Scaling by adding consumption is clumsy",
    body: "Adding machines, compute, or parameters just because it's possible is bad engineering. It's clumsy, and it hides the cost instead of paying it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Software is physical",
    body: "Digital doesn't mean clean. Every request costs energy, usually drawn from sources that aren't renewable. Treating that as abstract is how it stays expensive.",
  },
  {
    title: "Comfort is philosophical",
    body: "If starting a server needs a tree of dependencies, it isn't elegant, even when it's fast. I wanted a toolchain I could read end to end.",
  },
  {
    title: "Doing more with less is respect",
    body: "For the machine, for the energy, and for the people who don't have exceptional hardware to work on.",
  },
];

const method = [
  {
    title: "Name the machine",
    body: "Processor, cores, memory, and whether the box is shared. Run-to-run noise is expected on a shared machine, so medians of repeats get reported.",
  },
  {
    title: "Bracket every pair",
    body: "Before-and-after runs alternate A-B-A-B rather than measuring A fully and then B. A shared box drifts, and bracketing keeps the comparison honest.",
  },
  {
    title: "Report the failures",
    body: "Attempts that didn't pay off get published too, with the reason. A benchmark document that only lists wins isn't a measurement, it's marketing.",
  },
  {
    title: "Never fake energy",
    body: "CPU time is not joules. Without a hardware sensor, energy stays a proxy, labelled as one. No universal conversion factor gets applied.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Foundation"
  description="Why Orbit exists: scaling by adding consumption is clumsy, software is physical, and efficiency has to be measured before it's claimed. Built by Joaquín in Argentina."
>
  <Nav />

  <main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-8">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Foundation"
      heading="I'm building Orbit to see how far less can go."
      lead="The hypothesis is simple to state and awkward to prove: a server shouldn't need so much energy to be fast. I'm testing it, and this page is what I'm testing."
    />

    <Section class="pt-14 sm:pt-16" heading="The context">
      <div class="grid gap-8 border-t border-line pt-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr_1fr] lg:gap-14">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            {context.headline} {context.growth} That growth is the reason a language that compiles
            fast and needs little at runtime is worth building at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={context.sourceHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{context.source}</a>.
            It's context for the project, not a measurement of Orbit, and I'm not claiming any
            causal link between the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            Orbit is not an ecological project that happens to make a language. It's an engineering
            project whose natural result happens to be ecological. The engineering has to hold up
            first, on its own terms, or the rest is decoration.
          </p>
        </Prose>
        <div class="card p-6 sm:p-7">
          <p class="font-mono text-micro text-ink-subtle">Source</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-lead font-semibold text-ink">{context.headline}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-small text-ink-muted">{context.growth}</p>
          <p class="mt-5 text-micro text-ink-subtle">
            {context.source}. A third-party figure, quoted with its source rather than measured by
            me.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section class="pt-16 sm:pt-20" heading="What I believe about the trade">
      <div class="grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          beliefs.map((belief) => (
            <div class="bg-paper p-6 sm:p-7">
              <h3 class="text-subheading font-semibold text-balance text-ink">{belief.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-small leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{belief.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <Prose class="mt-8">
        <p>
          I have no interest in claiming Orbit beats a named language. Plenty of languages optimise
          for peak benchmark power, and that often needs more memory and energy to stay fast. Orbit
          tries a different trade, and whether it's a better one is something the measurements have
          to answer, not the marketing.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section
      class="pt-16 sm:pt-20"
      heading="How I measure"
      lead="The method is written down before the numbers, so I can't quietly change one to suit the other."
    >
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.05fr] lg:gap-12">
        <ol class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          {
            method.map((step, index) => (
              <li class="flex gap-4">
                <span class="mt-0.5 font-mono text-micro text-ink-subtle">
                  {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
                </span>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="text-small font-semibold text-ink">{step.title}</h3>
                  <p class="mt-1.5 text-small leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{step.body}</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <Callout variant="note" label="Where the method lives">
            <p>
              The full procedure, including the run counts, the discarded runs, and the rule about
              proxies, is in <a href={docLink("guides/benchmark-methodology.md")}>benchmark
              methodology</a> and <a href={docLink("ENERGY.md")}>energy measurement</a>.
            </p>
          </Callout>
          <Callout variant="measuring" label="What I don't know yet">
            <p>
              I don't know how far this goes. I don't have a clean measurement on a quiet machine
              yet, and the component-level wins I can isolate don't show up end to end, because the
              round trip is dominated by the loopback, the client, and the per-request access log.
            </p>
            <p>
              I'd rather say that plainly than put a round number on the home page. When the numbers
              are clean, they'll be here, with the bad runs included.
            </p>
          </Callout>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section class="pt-16 sm:pt-20" heading="Why it's built this way">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          The compiler is written in Orbit and compiles itself. That wasn't chosen for the logo; it
          was chosen because a toolchain you can't read is a toolchain you can't fix. The build
          reaches a fixed point, and a committed C file is the trust root, so verification doesn't
          need anything exotic.
        </p>
        <p>
          Orbit compiles to C, and your platform's C compiler produces the executable. One file per
          service, no runtime to install beside it. That's what "needs little" has to mean in
          practice, or it means nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project runs on one modest PC, with no team behind it. Every performance claim on this
          site has to survive that constraint, because it's the reason the project exists.
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <LinkButton href={docLink("architecture/SELF_HOSTING.md")} variant="secondary">
          How the bootstrap works
        </LinkButton>
        <LinkButton href={docLink("architecture/SOVEREIGNTY.md")} variant="tertiary">
          Trust and reproducibility
        </LinkButton>
        <LinkButton href={REPO.url} variant="tertiary">The repository</LinkButton>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <section class="mt-20 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-8 sm:p-10">
      <Prose>
        <h2>What I'm testing next</h2>
        <p>
          The honest next step isn't another transform. It's measuring the accept, parse, and
          send path on a machine I can account for, then deciding whether Superluminal is worth
          continuing at all. If the answer is no, I'll say so here and in the repository.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to follow that, the <a href={docLink("ROADMAP.md")}>roadmap</a> and{" "}
          <a href={docLink("STATUS.md")}>dated status</a> pages are the two places I update
          honestly, including the parts that didn't work.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
